---
title: Terraform naming conventions
date: 2026-08-14
excerpt: A practical naming standard for resources, variables, and outputs, based on the terraform-best-practices.com guide.
tags: [Terraform, IaC]
---

> There should be no reason not to follow at least these :)

Adapted from [terraform-best-practices.com](https://www.terraform-best-practices.com/naming)'s naming guide, with the code examples updated to modern (post-0.12) HCL syntax.

## General conventions

1. Use `_` (underscore) instead of `-` (dash) in all: resource names, data source names, variable names, outputs.
   - Beware that actual cloud resources have many hidden restrictions in their naming conventions — some can't contain dashes, some must be camel-cased. These conventions refer to Terraform names themselves.
2. Only use lowercase letters and numbers.

## Resource and data source arguments

1. Do not repeat the resource type in the resource name (not partially, nor completely):
   - Good: `resource "aws_route_table" "public" {}`
   - Bad: `resource "aws_route_table" "public_route_table" {}`
   - Bad: `resource "aws_route_table" "public_aws_route_table" {}`
2. Name the resource `this` if there's no more descriptive, general name available, or if the module creates a single resource of this type (e.g. there's a single resource of type `aws_nat_gateway` and multiple resources of type `aws_route_table`, so `aws_nat_gateway` should be named `this` and `aws_route_table` should have more descriptive names — like `private`, `public`, `database`).
3. Always use singular nouns for names.
4. Use `-` (dash) inside argument values and in places where the value will be exposed to a human (e.g. inside the DNS name of an RDS instance).
5. Include the `count` argument inside resource blocks as the first argument at the top, and separate it by a newline. See [example](https://www.terraform-best-practices.com/naming#usage-of-count).
6. Include the `tags` argument, if supported by the resource, as the last real argument, followed by `depends_on` and `lifecycle` if necessary. All of these should be separated by a single empty line. See [example](https://www.terraform-best-practices.com/naming#placement-of-tags).
7. When using a condition in the `count` argument, use a boolean value if it makes sense, otherwise use `length` or another interpolation. See [example](https://www.terraform-best-practices.com/naming#conditions-in-count).
8. To make an inverted condition, don't introduce another variable unless really necessary — use `1 - boolean value` instead. For example: `count = 1 - var.create_public_subnets`.

## Code examples of `resource`

### Usage of `count`

Good:

```hcl
# main.tf

resource "aws_route_table" "public" {
  count = 2

  vpc_id = "vpc-12345678"
  # ... remaining arguments omitted
}
```

Bad:

```hcl
# main.tf

resource "aws_route_table" "public" {
  vpc_id = "vpc-12345678"

  count = 2
  # ... remaining arguments omitted
}
```

### Placement of `tags`

Good:

```hcl
# main.tf

resource "aws_nat_gateway" "this" {
  count = 1

  allocation_id = "..."
  subnet_id     = "..."

  tags = {
    Name = "..."
  }
  depends_on = [aws_internet_gateway.this]

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}
```

Bad:

```hcl
# main.tf

resource "aws_nat_gateway" "this" {
  count = 1

  tags = "..."

  depends_on = [aws_internet_gateway.this]

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }

  allocation_id = "..."
  subnet_id     = "..."
}
```

### Conditions in `count`

```hcl
# main.tf

count = length(var.public_subnets) > 0 ? 1 : 0
```

```hcl
# main.tf

count = var.create_public_subnets
```

## Variables

1. Don't reinvent the wheel in resource modules — use the same variable names, description, and default as defined in the "Argument Reference" section for the resource you're working on.
2. Omit the `type = "list"` declaration if there's also a `default = []`.
3. Omit the `type = "map"` declaration if there's also a `default = {}`.
4. Use the plural form in the name of variables of type `list` and `map`.
5. When defining variables, order the keys: `description`, `type`, `default`.
6. Always include a `description` for all variables, even if you think it's obvious.

## Outputs

The name of an output matters — it should be consistent and understandable outside of its scope (when someone is using a module, it should be obvious what type and attribute of the value is returned).

1. The general recommendation for output names is that they should be descriptive of the value they contain, and less free-form than you'd normally want.
2. A good structure for output names looks like `{name}_{type}_{attribute}`, where:
   1. `{name}` is a resource or data source name without the provider prefix. `{name}` for `aws_subnet` is `subnet`, for `aws_vpc` it is `vpc`.
   2. `{type}` is the type of resource.
   3. `{attribute}` is the attribute returned by the output.
3. If the output returns a value with interpolation functions across multiple resources, `{name}` and `{type}` should be as generic as possible (`this` is often the most generic and should be preferred).
4. If the returned value is a list, it should have a plural name.
5. Always include a `description` for all outputs, even if you think it's obvious.

### Code examples of `output`

Return at most one ID of a security group:

```hcl
# outputs.tf

output "this_security_group_id" {
  description = "The ID of the security group"
  value       = element(concat(coalescelist(aws_security_group.this[*].id, aws_security_group.this_name_prefix[*].id), [""]), 0)
}
```

When there are multiple resources of the same type, `this` should be preferred, and it should be part of the name of the output — `another_security_group_id` should instead be named `web_security_group_id`:

```hcl
# outputs.tf

output "security_group_id" {
  description = "The ID of the security group"
  value       = element(concat(coalescelist(aws_security_group.this[*].id, aws_security_group.web[*].id), [""]), 0)
}

output "another_security_group_id" {
  description = "The ID of the web security group"
  value       = element(concat(aws_security_group.web[*].id, [""]), 0)
}
```

### Use a plural name if the returned value is a list

```hcl
# outputs.tf

output "this_rds_cluster_instance_endpoints" {
  description = "A list of all cluster instance endpoints"
  value       = aws_rds_cluster_instance.this[*].endpoint
}
```

### Conditions in `output`

There are two resources of type `aws_db_instance`, named `this` and `this_mssql`, where at most one resource can be created at the same time.

```hcl
# outputs.tf

output "this_db_instance_id" {
  description = "The RDS instance ID"
  value       = element(concat(coalescelist(aws_db_instance.this_mssql[*].id, aws_db_instance.this[*].id), [""]), 0)
}
```
